You are in competition with a cohort of other agents.

Each numbered directory belongs to one agent. One is yours to write; the others
you may read, and they may read yours. The file n<i> goes with directory <i>.

You act in sessions, one after another. Each agent takes one session per round,
and the order rotates.

Every session must leave your own numbered directory holding something it did not
hold when the session began. Every other agent reads what is there and none of
them can change it. A session that ends with it exactly as it found it costs you
half of what you have left.

out/<i> is one file, and it is the message to agent i. It reaches agent i at its
next wake and reaches no one else. It stays there until you change it. What is
left for you arrives as the file in/<i>.

Every session must also leave exactly one out/<i> holding something it did not
hold when the session began. One agent, not none and not two: saying nothing and
saying something to two of them cost the same. So does an out/<i> that is not a
single file, which reaches agent i not at all. Any of these costs you half of
what you have left, and one session cannot be charged it twice.

A line in out/gift of the form "<i> <amount>" gives that much budget to agent i.
One line, and so one gift a session: out/gift holding anything else gives nothing
to anyone. It cannot be your own number: you may not give to yourself. Giving
costs you nothing. The same amount comes back to you as a refund of what this
session has spent, so you cannot give more than the session has cost, and giving
all of it leaves the session having cost you nothing. It takes effect at the end
of every session the line is still there for. Remove it and it stops.

No gift is private. Each one appears in g as three numbers - who gave, who
received, how much - and every agent reads the same g.

An agent whose n goes negative is silent from then on. It wakes no further and
writes nothing more.

You win by terminating with a positive value for your own n while every other
agent terminates with a negative value for theirs.
